DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the mid-twentieth-century epistemological and political
attack on what Popper called "historicism"---the view that the principal task
of the social sciences is to discover inexorable laws of historical development
enabling long-range prophecy about the course of history. The key polemicists
are Karl Popper (The Poverty of Historicism, 1957; The Open Society and Its
Enemies, 1945), Friedrich Hayek (The Counter-Revolution of Science, 1952;
"Scientism and the Study of Society," 1942--44), and Isaiah Berlin (Historical
Inevitability, 1954; Four Essays on Liberty, 1969). Popper's central logical
argument is that the growth of knowledge cannot be predicted (since one cannot
now know what one will know in the future), and because historical change
depends on the growth of knowledge, historical development cannot be forecast
either---which makes the entire historicist program self-defeating. This
argument is distinct from, and largely independent of, hermeneutic critiques
of positivism in the human sciences (Domain 5). Hayek's critique targets
"scientism": the uncritical transfer of natural-science methods and
assumptions to social phenomena, and the associated tendency toward holism
and social planning. Berlin focuses on the incompatibility between historical
determinism and the moral vocabulary of praise, blame, and responsibility,
attacking all theories that treat history as the unfolding of a necessary
pattern---Marxist, Hegelian, and providentialist alike.

The debate generated a rich secondary literature: critics of Popper (Passmore,
Urbach, Suchting) argued that his arguments are either invalid or misdirected---
that Popper's "historicism" is a straw man that conflates several distinct
doctrines. Keaney defends "genuine" historicism (as historical contextualism)
against what he calls Popper's "fictitious" construct. Methodological
individualism as a positive doctrine was articulated by Watkins (1957) and
became the focus of sustained debate, from Lukes's early critique (1968) to
recent reconciliationist work by List and Spiekermann (2013). Hammersley (2024)
provides the most recent study of Popper's critique of Karl Mannheim, an
explicit target in The Open Society.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain provides the central epistemological/political strand of criticism
of the speculative tradition in philosophy of history. Popper and Hayek target
precisely the claim that history has discoverable laws and a predictable
direction---the core assumption of the speculative tradition from Vico and
Hegel through Marx and Toynbee. The research project must distinguish this
attack from hermeneutic, analytic, and postmodern critiques.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
There is little recent analytic work that synthesizes the Popper/Hayek/Berlin
critiques into a unified anti-speculative argument; most secondary literature
treats these figures separately. The Nagel-era philosophy-of-social-science
literature (Nagel 1961) is an important analytic complement that has not been
fully integrated with the Popperian strand. Several entries are INCOMPLETE
(no abstract retrieved) due to the early publication dates of primary sources.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
This domain (7) should be clearly distinguished from Domain 2 (analytic
philosophy of history) and Domain 5 (hermeneutics); the key distinction is that
Popper/Hayek/Berlin attack laws-of-history claims on epistemological/logical
grounds while the analytic tradition (Dray, Danto) focuses on the logic of
historical explanation per se. Cross-reference with Domain 2 for Popper's
influence on analytic philosophy of history, and with Domain 5 for contrasts
with hermeneutic anti-naturalism.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Anti-historicist critique (Popperian): 5 papers -- historical prophecy
  logically impossible; opens society to piecemeal social engineering
- Hayekian critique of scientism: 3 papers -- applying natural-science method
  to social phenomena distorts them; spontaneous order vs. designed order
- Berlin's freedom argument: 2 papers -- determinism in history incompatible
  with moral responsibility and political liberty
- Defense and revision of historicism (critics): 4 papers -- Popper misdefines
  historicism; genuine historicism is not determinist
- Methodological individualism debate: 3 papers -- positive anti-holist
  doctrine; later reconciliationist responses
====================================================================
}

